{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And they said, etc. — אֲבָל.</b> The word <b>אֲבָל</b> has no meaning here, and the Targumist rendered it ״בְּקוּשְׁטָא״ (in truth). And even regarding that rendering, I am inclined to ask why they needed to say ״בְּקוּשְׁטָא.״ <b>Further,</b> why did they elaborate in language to say <b>that we saw</b> etc., and not say it simply? And further, they should have said אֲשֶׁר מְכַרְנוּהוּ (that we sold him), which is the essence of the sin." "Or perhaps they intended to say the following: since they considered the possibility that this trouble was on account of the sale of Joseph, and they rejected that reason in accordance with what I have written (Genesis 37:20), that according to the law they had judged him to execution — and if so, the sale they effected for him was on the contrary an act of kindness. And if it was for having caused their father distress — on the contrary, that very thing would add him pain, and HaShem would not bring punishment for the sin of their father's distress through a thing that would further increase distress upon his distress. Therefore they found no [other] sin and said <b>אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים</b> etc. <b>אֲשֶׁר רָאִינוּ</b> etc. — meaning, in any case they should have had compassion on him when they saw their brother pleading for his life, and they were cruel to him; and this thing is reprehensible for the righteous to do. Therefore (it came upon us), etc." "Or it may intend along this line: since they all saw together that they had been brought to prison, they reasoned in their minds on whose account this evil had come. And since the sale of Joseph was as a sign between their eyes, when they sought to attribute it to him they found a contradiction to the matter on account of Reuben, who was seized with them yet was not among the sellers. Therefore they pressed and said <b>אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים</b> etc. — meaning, it is true that if we say the trouble came on account of the sale, Reuben poses a difficulty for us, for he did not sell; <b>אֲבָל אֲשֵׁמִים etc. אֲשֶׁר רָאִינוּ צָרַת נַפְשׁוֹ</b> etc., when they sought to lay hands on him. And in this matter Reuben was equal with them — he said (Genesis 37:22): ״הַשְׁלִיכוּ אֹתוֹ אֶל הַבּוֹר.״ And it is self-evident that Joseph would plead with them most intensely when being cast into the pit — because of the terror of the fall, the fumes of the pit, the snakes and scorpions, and also dying there of hunger and thirst, and there is no death as unusual as that. And he pleaded his voice to them yet they did not listen to the voice of his supplication, and in this Reuben's hand was with them. And that is the meaning of saying <b>עַל כֵּן בָּאָה אֵלֵינוּ</b> — meaning, together — the trouble. And all this is when we say that these words of theirs were when they placed them in prison; and the reason Scripture arranged their words afterward [in the narrative] is because He did not wish to interrupt in the midst of all of Joseph's words." "But if we say that these words of theirs were after they agreed to imprison one of them and they [the rest] would go up in peace, as Scripture arranges it, one must give a reason why they were not stirred to be concerned about their brother's guilt until now." "Or perhaps it is because beforehand they were not perceptive, and they thought that [the ruler] was a man with free choice and the will to do evil, and that it was not Joseph's sin causing it — for in their view he had been [rightly] condemned, as we explained in its place (Genesis 37:20). But when he said to them, ״אֲחִיכֶם אֶחָד יֵאָסֵר״ etc., ״וַיַּעֲשׂוּ כֵן״ — meaning: they themselves singled out one of them — by this they were roused from what appeared to them to be the sin of Joseph: just as they had been cruel to their brother, therefore this trouble came upon them in this manner, that one of them was imprisoned by the hand of a stranger. And I have already noted in several places that sin begets in its likeness, in its image, in the sod of the verse (Psalms 62:12): ״תְּשַׁלֵּם לְאִישׁ כְּמַעֲשֵׂהוּ,״ and so too the matter here — that it pained their hearts over their brother's distress. And that is the meaning of <b>אֲבָל</b> — meaning: if this occurrence had come about in a different way, we would not have justified [saying] that it came upon us on account of Joseph; אֲבָל now — surely we are guilty, that we saw the distress of his soul when he pleaded." "<b>And further</b>, there was a sign for them: since Shimon was set apart, as it is written (Genesis 42:24): ״וַיִּקַּח מֵאִתָּם אֶת שִׁמְעוֹן״ — from this they knew that it was the matter of Yoseif that surrounded him, for he was the one who counseled evil against Yosef, as it is written (Genesis 37:19): ״וַיֹּאמְרוּ אִישׁ אֶל אָחִיו״, and they of blessed memory said (Tanchuma, Vayechi) that they were Shimon and Levi."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:26.294468244Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}